>:The player doesn't own the level; in fact, he or she is not even native to
>:it, and it is in the nature of dwarves etc. to dig for minerals, gems etc. 
>:What harm does it do you for the level to be dug out (other than the display 
>:turning ugly)?
>
>     It makes it a lot more difficult to avoid swarms of monsters attacking
>you three or four at a time when whole segments of the level are opened up.
>Especially when you can only see one square at a time.  I've lost many a
>character to waiting in the middle of a room and having three soldier ants
>come up and massacre him (since they can move double-speed).  While that's
>generally my fault, peaceful dwarves could make that much more difficult to
>avoid.

I see your point. It is a good strategy for players to take on fast monsters
(or really anything that is dangerous) one at a time in doorways, and 
dwarf-digging makes that somewhat hard to do. 

I guess I have become somewhat fatalistic about dwarf-digging, though. To 
me, it's kind of a dungeon-level "entropy". It's also a strategy driver: as 
the danger of a monster swarm increases due to dwarf-digging, I have to 
continually reassess the value of staying on the level. Not that I usually 
linger on level without a good reason anyway (doing the old mega-sacrifice
bit I consider a good reason, as is the occasional vendetta with a nymph
who has stolen something valuable). Often I will just leave a level, not
because anything forced me off, or I was hungry, but because the topology 
was too dangerous. Dwarf-digging makes this a dynamic, rather than a static
strategy point.

In short, I think the interest and variety that dwarf-digging (actually,
all monster-digging) provides outweighs its "unfairness". And the player
(almost) always has the option of leaving the level in either direction if
it gets too dug-out.

(As a side note, there *are* times when I have welcomed the appearance of
a dwarf to chop away at a level. Most of these involve shops and cursed 
invisibility items...)

